# Managed MLflow on AWS

The end-to-end AWS tier can provision MLflow on a single Fargate task, PostgreSQL
on RDS and S3 artifacts. This is a self-hosted MLflow service on AWS-managed
infrastructure, not Amazon SageMaker Managed MLflow.

The task uses 1 vCPU and 4 GiB memory. A single task means restarts cause a short
interruption. PostgreSQL and S3 retain registry metadata and model artifacts across
restarts. API Gateway and its Lambda authorizer protect the private ALB path with
a bearer token stored in Secrets Manager. The internal ALB-to-container leg uses HTTP.

Set `AWS_MANAGED_MLFLOW=true` in the deployment environment. The deploy workflow
initializes the token only when no current secret version exists, then verifies
registry operations and artifact access. Never print tokens in logs or commit them.

```bash
gh workflow run verify-mlflow-aws.yml --ref main -f environment=dev
```

The verification creates a smoke model, restarts the MLflow task and checks existing
metadata and artifacts. The model-neutral smoke test does not train your business model.

For browser access from your authenticated workstation:

```bash
AWS_PROFILE=your-profile AWS_REGION=eu-north-1 \
uv run --extra aws python -m {{ package_name }}.aws.mlflow_ui_proxy
```

Open `http://127.0.0.1:5001`; keep the process running. This local process proxies the
remote MLflow UI and injects its bearer token. It does not host your registry database.

RDS, Fargate, ALBs and storage incur charges while provisioned. Destroy disposable
stacks after verification. RDS deletion protection must be deliberately disabled for
teardown; review snapshots, retained secrets, versioned S3 objects, ECR and the separately
created Terraform state bucket afterward. Keep backups if the models or metadata matter.
The state bucket must remain until all managed application resources have been removed.
